I found a viable Track B construction that stays in the paper’s native graph objects. Each instance is a succinctly labelled planar tree with two terminal pairs; the unique paths are planted by choosing endpoints in disjoint subtrees, then an affine relabelling hides binary-tree ancestry. Generic BFS is fully disclosed and measured; the compact route is to undo the relabelling and use ancestry. Difficulty grows by deepening off-route subtrees, so the two path certificates stay fixed-length while the mechanical search expands. I’m implementing and stress-testing that construction now.
